Original paper: https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/past_papers_for_natural_sciences/response/2258285/attach/3/FOI%202023%20126%20James%20materials%20bundle%201.pdf?cookie_passthrough=1#page=71[Released original PDF, physical pages 71–77].

= A1
{c}
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=A1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $D_x=y\partial_z-z\partial_y$, $D_y=z\partial_x-x\partial_z$ and $D_z=x\partial_y-y\partial_x$. The <product rule> applied to a smooth test <function> $f$ gives
$$
[D_x,D_y]f=y\partial_xf-x\partial_yf=-D_zf.
$$
The mixed second <partial derivatives> cancel because they commute. Since the <orbital angular momentum> components are $\hat j_i=-i\hbar D_i$, their <commutator> is
$$
\boxed{[\hat j_x,\hat j_y]=(-i\hbar)^2(-D_z)=i\hbar\hat j_z.}
$$
This is one of the <orbital angular momentum commutation relations>; the <Planck constant> enters through the definition of each <linear operator>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

<Cyclic permutation> of the coordinates in the preceding <commutator> calculation gives the remaining <orbital angular momentum commutation relations>:
$$
\boxed{[\hat j_y,\hat j_z]=i\hbar\hat j_x,\qquad [\hat j_z,\hat j_x]=i\hbar\hat j_y.}
$$
Reversing the order of a <commutator> reverses its sign.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use the <commutator derivation identity> $[A,BC]=[A,B]C+B[A,C]$ and the <orbital angular momentum commutation relations>. They give
$$
\begin{aligned}
[\hat j_z,\hat j_x^2]&=i\hbar(\hat j_y\hat j_x+\hat j_x\hat j_y),\\
[\hat j_z,\hat j_y^2]&=-i\hbar(\hat j_x\hat j_y+\hat j_y\hat j_x),\\
[\hat j_z,\hat j_z^2]&=0.
\end{aligned}
$$
The first two expressions cancel on addition, so
$$
\boxed{[\hat j_z,\hat{\mathbf j}^{\,2}]=0.}
$$
The same argument works for each component of the <angular momentum operator>.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The squared <angular momentum operator> and one selected component are <commuting operators>, so a complete set of common <eigenfunctions> can be chosen: for the $z$ component these are the usual $|j,m\rangle$ <angular momentum eigenstates>. Distinct components do not have a common complete <eigenbasis>, because their <commutator> is another nonzero component. A $j=0$ state is an exception in which all components vanish; noncommutation does not forbid every individual common <eigenfunction>.

= v
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

**The squared <angular momentum> and one chosen component can have simultaneously sharp values. All three components cannot have simultaneously sharp values in a nonzero-$j$ state.** The possible measured component values are $m\hbar$, while the squared magnitude is $j(j+1)\hbar^2$. This concerns the <quantum state>, rather than imperfect experimental resolution. The <Robertson uncertainty relation>, for example,
$$
\Delta j_x\,\Delta j_y\geq\frac{\hbar}{2}|\langle\hat j_z\rangle|,
$$
quantifies the restriction; its right-hand side may vanish in some states, without making all three components simultaneously sharp.

= b
{parent=A1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

It represents the **squared <orbital angular momentum>**. In <atomic units>, $\hbar=1$, so its <eigenvalues> are $l(l+1)$. In ordinary units the corresponding observable is $\hbar^2l(l+1)$, with <orbital angular momentum quantum number> $l=0,1,2,\ldots$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <spherical harmonic> is also an <eigenfunction> of the $z$ component of <orbital angular momentum>:
$$
\boxed{\hat l_zY_{lm}=mY_{lm},\qquad m=-l,-l+1,\ldots,l.}
$$
These are <atomic units>; restoring units gives $m\hbar$. The integer $m$ is the <magnetic quantum number>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

<Separation of variables> makes the angular factor a <spherical harmonic>. Applying the <quantum Hamiltonian> gives
$$
\hat H(RY_{lm})=
Y_{lm}\left[-\frac{1}{2r^2}\frac{d}{dr}\left(r^2R'\right)
+\frac{l(l+1)}{2r^2}R-\frac{R}{r}\right].
$$
Consequently $RY_{lm}$ is an <energy eigenfunction> **provided the radial factor satisfies**
$$
\boxed{-\frac{1}{2r^2}\frac{d}{dr}(r^2R')+
\left[\frac{l(l+1)}{2r^2}-\frac1r\right]R=ER.}
$$
This is the <radial Schrödinger equation for a central potential>, here with the attractive <Coulomb potential>. An arbitrary $R$ does not give an <eigenfunction>; regularity, decay and <wavefunction normalization> select admissible bound <quantum states>.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For a $2p$ <atomic orbital>, $l=1$. The radial factor obeys
$$
R'=Ne^{-r/2}(1-r/2),\qquad R''=Ne^{-r/2}(r/4-1).
$$
Substitution into the <radial Schrödinger equation for a central potential> gives
$$
-\frac12R''-\frac{R'}r+\frac{R}{r^2}-\frac Rr=-\frac18R.
$$
Thus the <energy eigenvalue> is
$$
\boxed{E=-\frac18\,E_{\rm h}\simeq-3.40\ \mathrm{eV}.}
$$
The <normalization constant> cancels; $E_{\rm h}$ is the <Hartree energy>.

= A2
{c}
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=A2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <quantum variational principle> says that every admissible nonzero trial <wavefunction> obeys
$$
\boxed{\widetilde E=
\frac{\langle\widetilde\Psi|\hat H|\widetilde\Psi\rangle}
{\langle\widetilde\Psi|\widetilde\Psi\rangle}\geq E_0,}
$$
where $E_0$ is the <ground-state energy>. Thus the <Rayleigh quotient> is an upper bound. Equality holds when the trial lies entirely in the <ground-state subspace>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Expand the trial in a complete <orthonormal eigenbasis> of the <quantum Hamiltonian>, $\widetilde\Psi=\sum_nc_n\phi_n$, with $\hat H\phi_n=E_n\phi_n$. <Orthonormality> gives
$$
\widetilde E=
\frac{\sum_n|c_n|^2E_n}{\sum_n|c_n|^2},\qquad
\widetilde E-E_0=
\frac{\sum_n|c_n|^2(E_n-E_0)}{\sum_n|c_n|^2}\geq0.
$$
Every summand in the numerator is nonnegative because $E_n\geq E_0$. This proves the <quantum variational principle> for the stated discrete expansion; the trial <energy> is a <weighted mean> of exact <energy eigenvalues>.

= b
{parent=A2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Inside the <particle in a box>, the <quantum Hamiltonian> is
$$
\boxed{\hat H=-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\frac{d^2}{dx^2},\qquad 0<x<a.}
$$
The infinite potential outside imposes the <Dirichlet boundary conditions> $\psi(0)=\psi(a)=0$ and a zero <wavefunction> outside the interval. These boundary conditions are part of the <Hamiltonian operator>, rather than an optional addition.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Twice <differentiation> the sine gives
$$
\hat H\sin\frac{n\pi x}{a}
=\frac{n^2\pi^2\hbar^2}{2ma^2}\sin\frac{n\pi x}{a}.
$$
Both <Dirichlet boundary conditions> hold when $n$ is an integer. The zero function at $n=0$ is not an <eigenfunction>, and negative $n$ merely changes an overall sign. Therefore
$$
\boxed{n=1,2,\ldots,\qquad E_n=\frac{n^2\pi^2\hbar^2}{2ma^2}.}
$$
The corresponding normalized <eigenfunction> is $\sqrt{2/a}\sin(n\pi x/a)$.

= c
{parent=A2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The trial already vanishes at $x=0$. The second <Dirichlet boundary condition> requires $a(a-c)=0$, hence **$c=a$** for a box of positive length. This makes the trial an admissible <particle in a box> <wavefunction>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Up to a <global phase>, $x(a-x)$ is real, positive throughout the interior, has no interior <nodes of a wavefunction>, and is symmetric about $x=a/2$. It is smooth inside the box and has one central maximum. These are the shape and <reflection symmetry> properties of the lowest <particle in a box> <eigenfunction>, $\sin(\pi x/a)$; they make it a plausible <variational method> trial without making it exact.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Since
$$
\int_0^a x^2(a-x)^2\,dx=\frac{a^5}{30},
$$
an explicitly normalized trial is
$$
\boxed{\widetilde\psi(x)=\sqrt{\frac{30}{a^5}}\,x(a-x),\qquad 0<x<a.}
$$
It is zero outside the box. The original expression $x(x-a)$ differs by a minus sign, which changes only the <global phase> and leaves <probabilities> and <expectation values> unchanged.

= iv
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

<Integration by parts> and the zero endpoint values turn the <Rayleigh quotient> into a positive <kinetic energy> integral:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\widetilde E&=\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\int_0^a|\widetilde\psi'(x)|^2\,dx\\
&=\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\frac{30}{a^5}\int_0^a(a-2x)^2\,dx
=\boxed{\frac{5\hbar^2}{ma^2}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
This is the <parabolic variational trial for a particle in a box>. The <potential energy> vanishes inside the box.

= v
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Comparing the <variational method> estimate with the exact <ground-state energy> gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{\widetilde E}{E_1}=\frac{10}{\pi^2}=1.01321\ldots>1.}
$$
The estimate is about **$1.32\%$ too high**, consistently with the <quantum variational principle>. Its closeness does not mean that the trial <wavefunction> is an exact <energy eigenfunction>.

= vi
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}

The trial is even under reflection about the midpoint. The normalized box <eigenfunctions> satisfy $\phi_n(a-x)=(-1)^{n+1}\phi_n(x)$, so <orthogonality> between even and odd functions gives $c_n=0$ for even $n$. If the higher odd contributions $n\geq5$ are neglected, the <weighted mean> from the <quantum variational principle> becomes
$$
\widetilde E\simeq
\frac{|c_1|^2E_1+|c_3|^2E_3}{|c_1|^2+|c_3|^2}.
$$
Rearranging yields the <two-level variational contamination estimate>
$$
\boxed{\frac{|c_3|^2}{|c_1|^2}\simeq
\frac{\widetilde E-E_1}{E_3-\widetilde E}.}
$$
The required assumption concerns the neglected contributions to the <energy> as well as their small probabilities. Numerically the two-level estimate is $0.0016542$. It is not an exact identity: direct expansion of this parabolic trial gives $c_n\propto n^{-3}$ for odd $n$, hence the exact ratio is $1/729=0.0013717$. The omitted higher odd states account for the difference.

= A3
