= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Place the <carbon> and <sulfur> nuclei on a line separated by $r$, with masses $m_{\rm C}$ and $m_{\rm S}$. The <center of mass> lies between them and closer to the heavier <sulfur> nucleus. In the <linear rigid rotor> approximation, $r$ remains fixed while the <molecule> rotates.

\Image[paper-1-carbon-monosulfide-rotor.png]
{title=Original <carbon monosulfide> rotor diagram showing masses, bond length and distances from the <center of mass>}

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Measure $x$ from the <carbon> nucleus toward the <sulfur> nucleus. The definition of the <center of mass> gives
$$
x_{\rm cm}=\frac{m_{\rm C}\,0+m_{\rm S}r}{m_{\rm C}+m_{\rm S}}.
$$
Therefore the requested distance is
$$
\boxed{r_1=\frac{m_{\rm S}}{m_{\rm C}+m_{\rm S}}r.}
$$
The other distance is $r_2=r-r_1=m_{\rm C}r/(m_{\rm C}+m_{\rm S})$, consistent with $m_{\rm C}r_1=m_{\rm S}r_2$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For an axis through the <center of mass> perpendicular to the bond, the point-mass <moment of inertia> is
$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=m_{\rm C}r_1^2+m_{\rm S}r_2^2\\
&=\frac{m_{\rm C}m_{\rm S}^2+m_{\rm S}m_{\rm C}^2}
{(m_{\rm C}+m_{\rm S})^2}r^2
=\boxed{\frac{m_{\rm C}m_{\rm S}}{m_{\rm C}+m_{\rm S}}r^2}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The prefactor is the <reduced mass> $\mu$; this is the standard reduction of a two-body <linear rigid rotor> to $I=\mu r^2$.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Using the stipulated integer <isotope> masses gives
$$
\mu=\frac{12\times32}{44}u=8.72727u,\qquad
I=\mu(1.53\times10^{-10}\ \mathrm m)^2
=3.39243\times10^{-46}\ \mathrm{kg\,m^2}.
$$
The <rotational constant> in <wavenumber> units is
$$
\boxed{\widetilde B=\frac{h}{8\pi^2cI}=0.8252\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}.}
$$
Here $c$ must be expressed in $\mathrm{cm\,s^{-1}}$ when $I$ is in SI units. Equivalently calculate $B=h/(8\pi^2I)$ in $\mathrm{Hz}$ first, then divide by $c$ in $\mathrm{cm\,s^{-1}}$.

= b
{parent=A3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <rotational state populations> start at $N_0/N_0=1$. Their $2J+1$ <statistical weight> factor initially favors higher $J$, while the <Boltzmann factor> eventually suppresses them, producing one maximum followed by a tail tending to zero. The physically allowed values occur at integer $J$; a continuous curve only guides the eye.

\Image[paper-1-carbon-monosulfide-populations.png]
{title=Original <linear rigid rotor> populations for <carbon monosulfide> at $300\ \mathrm K$ and $8.7\ \mathrm K$}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Set $\theta_{\rm rot}=hc\widetilde B/k_{\rm B}$, the <rotational temperature>. For a continuous nonnegative variable $J$,
$$
\frac{d}{dJ}\log\frac{N_J}{N_0}
=\frac{2}{2J+1}-\frac{\theta_{\rm rot}}T(2J+1).
$$
Equating this <derivative> to zero gives the <most populated rotational level>
$$
\boxed{J_*=\frac12\left(\sqrt{\frac{2T}{\theta_{\rm rot}}}-1\right)
=\sqrt{\frac{k_{\rm B}T}{2hc\widetilde B}}-\frac12.}
$$
The second <derivative> is $-4/(2J+1)^2-2\theta_{\rm rot}/T<0$, so this is a maximum when $J_*\geq0$. At lower temperatures the constrained continuous maximum is $J=0$. For the physical integer maximum compare the nearby allowed levels explicitly.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The calculated <rotational constant> gives $\theta_{\rm rot}=1.18721\ \mathrm K$. At room <temperature>, taken as $300\ \mathrm K$, the continuous maximum is $J_*=10.7404$; comparing the adjacent integer populations gives **$J_{\max}=11$**. Taking $298.15\ \mathrm K$ instead gives $J_*=10.7057$ and the same integer answer.

At $8.7\ \mathrm K$, $J_*=1.41417$. In particular,
$$
\frac{N_2}{N_1}=\frac53e^{-4\theta_{\rm rot}/T}=0.966\ldots<1,
$$
and $N_1>N_0$, so **$J_{\max}=1$**. The integer comparison avoids treating a noninteger <angular momentum quantum number> as a physical state.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Low <rotational energy> spacings can be excited in cold interstellar gas when electronic and most vibrational excitations are frozen out. A polar <molecule> such as <carbon monosulfide> has allowed <electric dipole transition> lines whose frequencies depend on its <moment of inertia>, providing a distinctive pattern for <rotational spectral line assignment>. Several matching <spectral lines> and their <isotope shifts> give a much stronger identification than one coincidence. The method is especially effective for polar species; a molecule without a permanent <electric dipole moment> lacks an ordinary pure electric-dipole <rotational spectrum>.

= A4
